# Performance Validation Rule

## Purpose & Scope

Defines comprehensive performance validation standards for application code. This rule ensures code follows performance best practices, meets performance requirements, and is optimized for production environments across multiple programming languages.

**Applies to**: All source code files before code review, merge, or deployment

**Does not apply to**: Configuration files, documentation, test data, or build artifacts

## Inputs (Contract)

- Source code files in supported programming languages
- Performance testing tools and frameworks
- Benchmarking tools
- Profiling tools
- Performance requirements and SLAs

## Outputs (Contract)

- Performance metrics and benchmarks
- Profiling results and analysis
- Performance validation checklist
- Bottleneck identification and recommendations
- Performance regression detection
- Performance validation evidence

## Core Performance Principles

**PERFORMANCE IS A FEATURE, NOT AN AFTERTHOUGHT.**

**MEASURE BEFORE OPTIMIZING - Don't optimize without data.**

**PREMATURE OPTIMIZATION IS THE ROOT OF ALL EVIL - Focus on critical paths.**

**SCALABILITY IS KEY - Code must handle growth gracefully.**

## Mandatory Performance Gates

### Gate 1: Algorithmic Efficiency (CRITICAL)

#### Time Complexity Analysis
- [ ] No O(n²) algorithms where O(n) exists
- [ ] Proper data structures used
- [ ] Efficient sorting algorithms
- [ ] Optimal search algorithms
- [ ] Proper caching strategies

#### Space Complexity Analysis
- [ ] Memory usage within acceptable limits
- [ ] No memory leaks detected
- [ ] Proper resource management
- [ ] Efficient data structures
- [ ] Proper garbage collection

#### Algorithmic Best Practices
- [ ] No unnecessary computations
- [ ] Early exits in loops
- [ ] Proper memoization
- [ ] Efficient string operations
- [ ] Proper use of built-in functions

### Gate 2: Database and I/O Performance (CRITICAL)

#### Database Optimization
- [ ] Proper indexing strategies
- [ ] Efficient query patterns
- [ ] No N+1 query problems
- [ ] Proper connection pooling
- [ ] Database query optimization

#### I/O Operations
- [ ] Efficient file operations
- [ ] Proper buffering strategies
- [ ] Asynchronous I/O where appropriate
- [ ] Network request optimization
- [ ] Proper error handling in I/O

#### Caching Strategies
- [ ] Appropriate caching levels
- [ ] Cache invalidation strategies
- [ ] Cache size management
- [ ] Cache hit ratio monitoring
- [ ] Distributed caching where needed

### Gate 3: Code and Architecture Performance (HIGHLY RECOMMENDED)

#### Code Optimization
- [ ] No unnecessary computations
- [ ] Proper use of language features
- [ ] Efficient data structures
- [ ] Proper algorithm selection
- [ ] Code refactoring for performance

#### Architecture Performance
